\n            \"explanation\": \"Insulation Displacement Connectors (IDC) are a key technology employed within 'TOMADA DE REDE' (Network Jacks). IDC technology is designed to pierce the insulation of wires, creating a direct electrical contact. This method simplifies the termination process for network cables, eliminating the need for stripping wires or soldering. The integration of IDC connectors streamlines the installation and maintenance of network jacks [Data: Entities (1660), Relationships (2696)].\\n\\nBy utilizing IDC, the connection between the horizontal cabling and the network jack is made efficiently and reliably. This technology is specifically mentioned in the context of patch panels as well, indicating its broader application in network infrastructure termination. The reliance on IDC highlights a focus on ease of installation and robust connections within the network.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Component Lifespan is a Standardized Consideration\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Vida Útil dos Componentes' (Component Lifespan) is an important aspect related to network jacks. This refers to the expected durability and longevity of cabling components, including the number of times a jack can be plugged into and unplugged from (insertions/removals), and the overall expected lifespan of network jacks. Standards bodies typically specify these metrics, ensuring a certain level of performance and reliability over time [Data: Entities (1662), Relationships (2700)].\\n\\nConsidering the lifespan is crucial for network planning, maintenance, and budgeting. It informs decisions about when components might need replacement and helps in designing networks that are sustainable in the long term. The fact that this is a standardized consideration underscores its importance in the structured cabling industry.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Horizontal Cabling as the Backbone to Network Jacks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Cabelo Horizontal' (Horizontal Cabling) refers to the network infrastructure that runs horizontally throughout a building, connecting telecommunications rooms to the user areas. This cabling forms the backbone that terminates at the 'TOMADA DE REDE' (Network Jack) in the workstation areas. The relationship between horizontal cabling and network jacks is fundamental, as the jacks are the designated endpoints for this infrastructure [Data: Entities (1664), Relationships (2608, 2703)].\\n\\nEffectively, the horizontal cabling provides the pathway for data transmission, and the network jack provides the accessible point for devices to tap into this pathway. The integrity and quality of the horizontal cabling directly impact the performance and reliability of the connections made at the network jacks. This indicates a hierarchical structure within the network setup, where horizontal cabling enables the functionality of the termination points.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"End-User Devices Connect via Network Jacks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Dispositivo do Usuário' (End-User Device) is the component that ultimately benefits from the network infrastructure described. These are the devices, such as computers or other network-enabled equipment, that are connected to the 'TOMADA DE REDE' (Network Jack) to access the network. This relationship represents the final step in the wired network connection process [Data: Entities (1671), Relationships (2719)].\\n\\nThe network jack, supported by IDC technology and horizontal cabling, provides the necessary physical interface for these end-user devices. The description emphasizes that a user device is connected to a network jack, highlighting the jack's role as the gateway for devices to join the network. This underscores the user-centric purpose of these networking components.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 3.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is low 